Security Reflection
Using the MySQL root account directly for application backend connections is poor security practice because the root account has full privileges over the database server. If the application is compromised, an attacker could use these privileges to modify or delete important data. A dedicated application user with only the required permissions is safer and follows the principle of least privilege.
CREATE USER 'school_app'@'localhost'
IDENTIFIED BY 'StrongPasswordHere';

GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE
ON school.*
TO 'school_app'@'localhost';

FLUSH PRIVILEGES;


